course: dictionary
lesson: 13487
title: Idolise: admiring someone very much
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: To idolise someone is to admire or love them very much, so much that you see them as nearly perfect. Fans idolise footballers and singers. «Mia idolises her big sister and copies everything she does.» Mia thinks her sister is wonderful, and she wants to be just like her.
explain: Idolise is a verb, so it changes: idolise, idolises, idolised, idolising. In American English the spelling is idolize. To check the meaning, look at the clues: if someone copies a person, talks about them all the time and loves them, they probably idolise them.
explain: Idolise has a second meaning, from religion. To idolise something is to treat it as an idol and worship it. Jews, Christians and Muslims teach that worshipping idols is wrong, so this meaning is about belief, not about liking a film star.
fact: Idolise is a verb: idolise, idolises, idolised, idolising.
fact: «Idolise» means to admire or love someone very much, as if they were perfect.
fact: The US spelling is idolize.
fact: «A hero he idolised» means a hero he admired very much.
fact: In religion, to idolise something means to treat it as an idol and worship it, and Jews, Christians and Muslims teach that this is wrong.
exercise: «Ben idolises his favourite footballer and wears the same shirt.» What does idolises mean? | *Admires someone so much he seems nearly perfect | Is angry with someone who lost a match | Ignores someone completely | Forgets someone's name
exercise: «Leo idolised the striker, but he learns the striker sometimes makes mistakes.» Find and copy the word that means admired very much. | striker | mistakes | sometimes | *idolised
exercise: «The villagers idolised a statue of their god.» What does idolised mean here? | Sold the statue at a fair | Forgot the statue was there | *Treated the statue as an idol and worshipped it | Painted the statue a bright colour
test: «The fans idolise the singer and know all her songs.» What does idolise mean? | Dislike her singing | *Admire her so much she seems perfect | Have never heard of her | Sing her songs badly
test: Which is the US spelling of idolise? | *idolize | idolice | idolyse | idolist
exam: Amir idolised the hero, but later he saw that the hero had flaws. What does this show about idolise? | *Admiring someone so much that you see them as perfect, so their flaws come as a shock | Ignoring someone's mistakes on purpose | Becoming a hero yourself | Looking up at the sky
